* {{IPA|en|/əˈkʌm.plɪʃ/|/əˈkɒm.plɪʃ/|a=RP}}<ref>The Chambers Dictionary, 9th Ed., 2003</ref><ref>{{R:Collins English Dictionary}}</ref><ref>{{R:Lexico}}</ref>
* {{IPA|en|/əˈkɑm.plɪʃ/|a=GA}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈkɒm.plɪʃ/|a=Canada}}
* {{IPA|en|/əˈkɐm.plɪʃ/|a=NZ,Aus}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-accomplish.ogg|a=US}}
* {{hyphenation|en|ac|com|plish}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[finish]] [[successful]]ly.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[complete]], as [[time]] or [[distance]].
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Daniel|9|2|passage=That He would '''accomplish''' seventy years in the desolations of Jerusalem.}}
#* {{RQ:Prescott Philip 2|volume=I|chapter=War with France|page=231|passage=But the rising ground which lay between him and the French prevented him from seeing the enemy until he had '''accomplished''' half a league or more.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[execute]] [[fully]]; to [[fulfill]]; to complete successfully.
#: {{co|en|to '''accomplish''' a design, an object, a promise}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Luke|22|37|passage=This that is written must yet be '''accomplished''' in me}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To [[equip]] or [[furnish]] [[thoroughly]]; hence, to complete in [[acquirement]]s; to [[render]] accomplished; to [[polish]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|act=IV|scene=|passage=The armorers '''accomplishing''' the knights}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1638|author=w:John Wilkins|title=The Discovery of a World in the Moone
|passage=It [the moon] is fully '''accomplished''' for all those ends to which Providence did appoint it.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1863|author=w:Charles Cowden Clarke|title=Shakespeare's Characters
|passage=These qualities . . . go to '''accomplish''' a perfect woman.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|obsolete}} To [[gain]]; to [[obtain]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-3|act=III|scene=ii|passage=And more unlikely / Than to '''accomplish''' twenty golden crowns!}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|Philippines}} To [[fill out]] a [[form]].

